    "q-79326": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79326",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$\n\nII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4$\n\nIII. $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=5$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by the vectors by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Hence,$w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ this means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\}$ is completely spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=4$ (not $5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
